 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T12:45+08:00",
 "answer": "Yes, you can generally go jogging in Penang, though you should take a few precautions depending on your sensitivity and the midday heat.\n\n**Current Air Quality in Penang:**\n* **Status:** **Moderate** ( bordering on *Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups* in some areas)\n* **AQI Range:** **73 to 95** (with daily highs forecasted around 102)\n* **Primary Pollutant:** Fine particulate matter (PM2.5)\n* **Breakdown by Area:** \n  * **George Town:** ~86–95 AQI\n  * **Batu Ferringhi:** ~83 AQI\n  * **Perai (Mainland):** ~73–82 AQI\n\n**What this means for your jog:**\n* **For most people:** The air quality is acceptable for outdoor exercise. You are fine to go for a run, though you may want to keep it at a moderate pace rather than doing prolonged, high-intensity interval training if the air feels slightly hazy.\n* **For sensitive groups:** Because the AQI in George Town is approaching 100, if you have asthma, respiratory issues, or are unusually sensitive to air pollution, it is best to reduce prolonged or heavy exertion outdoors (or opt for an indoor treadmill today). \n\n**Weather Factor:**\nKeep in mind that it is right around midday (12:45 PM local time) in Penang, with temperatures hitting around **30°C–31°C (86°F–88°F)** and high humidity. The combination of midday tropical heat and high-moderate PM2.5 levels can make breathing feel heavier than usual. If possible, waiting until late afternoon or early evening—and sticking to shaded, greener areas like the Botanical Gardens or Youth Park—will make for a much more comfortable run.",
